Here's a complete example of an MCP server wrapping a REST API:

---

## Tool Class (`ApiTools.cs`)

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class ApiTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<ApiTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get a user by their ID from the REST API.")]
    public async Task<string> GetUser(
        [Description("The numeric user ID to look up")] int userId,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching user {UserId}", userId);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/users/{userId}", cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Create a new user in the system.")]
    public async Task<string> CreateUser(
        [Description("The user's full name")] string name,
        [Description("The user's email address")] string email,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var payload = JsonSerializer.Serialize(new { name, email });
        var content = new StringContent(payload, System.Text.Encoding.UTF8, "application/json");
        var response = await httpClient.PostAsync("/users", content, cancellationToken);
        response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
        return await response.Content.ReadAsStringAsync(cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

---

## `Program.cs` (stdio transport)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the REST API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK integrates directly with `Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection`:

| Mechanism | How it works |
|-----------|-------------|
| **Constructor injection** | Non-static `[McpServerToolType]` classes are resolved from DI. Use primary constructors (as above) to receive `HttpClient`, `ILogger<T>`, etc. |
| **Method parameter injection** | On static classes, DI services can appear as method parameters. The SDK resolves them automatically — they **don't** appear in the tool's JSON schema. |
| **Auto-injected types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>`, and `RequestContext<T>` are always available without registration. |

**Key rule:** Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed in the tool schema to the LLM. Everything else (DI services, `CancellationToken`, etc.) is injected invisibly.

**Registration in Program.cs:**
- `AddHttpClient<ApiTools>(...)` — registers a typed `HttpClient` for the tool class
- `WithToolsFromAssembly()` — discovers all `[McpServerToolType]` classes via reflection
- `ILogger<T>` — available by default, no extra registration needed

This gives you full testability (mock the `HttpClient`/services) while keeping tool methods focused on business logic.
